1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,227
This is ECE 320, video 2. The topic is performance.

2
00:00:05,677 --> 00:00:26,754
After this video, you can solve four types of problems. You can calculate the CPI from an instruction mix. You can calculate and compare execution times. You can use Amdahl's law to calculate speedup. And you can work backwards in Amdahl's law. Every past midterm starts with a question of this type.

3
00:00:27,204 --> 00:00:37,124
There are two measures of performance. The first is latency. Latency is the time to finish one fixed task. For latency, smaller is better.

4
00:00:37,574 --> 00:00:45,510
The second is throughput. Throughput is the number of tasks that finish in a fixed time. For throughput, larger is better.

5
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:53,043
Most questions in this course use latency. That is, the execution time of one program, in seconds.

6
00:00:53,493 --> 00:01:16,106
The CPU performance equation gives the execution time of a program. Time equals instructions per program, times cycles per instruction, times seconds per cycle. The picture shows a small program of 5 instructions. Each blue box is one instruction. The dashed lines split an instruction into its clock cycles.

7
00:01:16,556 --> 00:01:32,449
The first term is the instruction count, I C. It is the dynamic count. That is the number of instructions that actually execute, with each loop pass counted again. Here it is 5. The program, the compiler and the ISA set it.

8
00:01:32,899 --> 00:01:49,539
The second term is CPI, the average number of cycles per instruction. Here 8 cycles for 5 instructions gives a CPI of 1.6. Its inverse is I P C. The compiler and the micro-architecture set it.

9
00:01:49,989 --> 00:02:08,293
The third term is the clock cycle time, the length of one cycle. It is one divided by the clock frequency. So this program takes 5 times 1.6 times t clock, which is 8 cycle times. The micro-architecture and the physical layout set it.

10
00:02:08,743 --> 00:02:21,991
To get a short execution time, you must make all three terms small. In practice, a change that makes one term smaller often makes another term larger. So always use the full equation.

11
00:02:22,441 --> 00:02:36,244
Different instructions take different numbers of cycles. So the CPI of a program is a weighted average. Multiply the fraction of each instruction type by its CPI, and add the results.

12
00:02:36,694 --> 00:02:52,950
Here is the example from slide 4. Program A has equal numbers of integer, floating point, and memory instructions. Integer instructions take 1 cycle, memory instructions take 2, and floating point instructions take 3.

13
00:02:53,400 --> 00:03:11,427
Each type is one third of the instructions. So the CPI is one third times 1, plus one third times 2, plus one third times 3. That is 6 divided by 3, which is 2. The bar shows each term. The slow type adds the most cycles.

14
00:03:11,877 --> 00:03:16,997
Remember that the fractions are fractions of instructions, not fractions of time.

15
00:03:17,447 --> 00:03:46,332
Here is the worked example from slide 6. The table gives the instruction mix. Option A makes branches cost 1 cycle. Option B adds a cache, so loads cost 3 cycles. Which option is better? First, find the base CPI. Point 5 times 1, plus point 2 times 5, plus point 1 times 1, plus point 2 times 2. The base CPI is 2. The bar shows the cycles that each type adds. Loads add the most.

16
00:03:46,782 --> 00:03:54,227
For option A, change only the branch row. The branch cost becomes 1. The CPI becomes 1.8.

17
00:03:54,677 --> 00:04:02,379
For option B, change only the load row. The load cost becomes 3. The CPI becomes 1.6.

18
00:04:02,829 --> 00:04:14,050
Option B gives the lower CPI. The instruction count and the clock do not change. So the lower CPI gives the shorter time, and option B is better.

19
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:30,287
The reason: each load saves 2 cycles, but each branch saves only 1 cycle. Both types are 20 percent of the instructions. Method for this question type: change one row, recalculate the weighted sum, and compare.

20
00:04:30,737 --> 00:04:53,905
Many people think that a higher clock frequency means a faster processor. Slide 8 shows why this is wrong. Processor A has a CPI of 2 and a 5 gigahertz clock. Processor B has a CPI of 1 and a 3 gigahertz clock. Both use the same ISA and compiler, so the instruction count is the same.

21
00:04:54,355 --> 00:05:06,131
Because the instruction count is the same, compare the time per instruction. That is the CPI times the cycle time, or the CPI divided by the frequency.

22
00:05:06,581 --> 00:05:25,887
For A, 2 divided by 5 gigahertz is point 4 nanoseconds. For B, 1 divided by 3 gigahertz is point 3 3 3 nanoseconds. The picture draws one instruction to scale. A has two short cycles. B has one longer cycle, but the total is shorter.

23
00:05:26,337 --> 00:05:35,703
B takes less time per instruction. So B is faster, by point 4 divided by point 3 3 3, which is 1.2 times.

24
00:05:36,153 --> 00:05:42,147
Rule: do not compare clock frequency alone. Compare the product of all three terms.

25
00:05:42,597 --> 00:06:04,187
Now a practice question from assignment 1, question 4 b. Machine A has a 20 nanosecond cycle and a CPI of 1.5. Machine B has a 15 nanosecond cycle and a CPI of 1.0. Which machine is faster, and by how much? Pause the video and try it first.

26
00:06:04,637 --> 00:06:12,445
Both machines run the same program on the same ISA. So the instruction count is the same, and it cancels.

27
00:06:12,895 --> 00:06:19,679
Machine A takes I C times 1.5 times 20. That is 30 I C nanoseconds.

28
00:06:20,129 --> 00:06:27,169
Machine B takes I C times 1.0 times 15. That is 15 I C nanoseconds.

29
00:06:27,619 --> 00:06:34,168
The ratio is 30 divided by 15, which is 2. So machine B is 2 times faster.

30
00:06:34,618 --> 00:06:43,194
Remember the meaning of times faster. X is n times faster than Y when the time of Y divided by the time of X is n.

31
00:06:43,644 --> 00:07:04,295
This practice question is from assignment 1, question 3. Here the instruction count changes too. CPU A uses a separate compare instruction before each branch. CPU B does the compare inside the branch. CPU A has a faster clock. Which CPU is faster? Pause and try it.

32
00:07:04,745 --> 00:07:22,985
Use the instruction count of A as the base. For A, compares are point 2 with 1 cycle, branches are point 2 with 2 cycles, and others are point 6 with 1 cycle. The sum is 1.2. So the time of A is 1.2 times I C A times t A.

33
00:07:23,435 --> 00:07:44,149
CPU B has no compare instructions. So it runs only point 8 of the instructions of A. Keep the counts per I C A: branches point 2 times 2, plus others point 6 times 1. The sum is 1.0. So the time of B is 1.0 times I C A times t B.

34
00:07:44,599 --> 00:07:57,314
In part a, the clock cycle of B is 1.25 times longer. So the time of B is 1.25. This is more than 1.2, so CPU A is faster.

35
00:07:57,764 --> 00:08:10,223
In part b, the clock cycle of B is 1.1 times longer. So the time of B is 1.1. This is less than 1.2, so CPU B is faster.

36
00:08:10,673 --> 00:08:23,046
The common mistake is to rescale the mix of B to 25 and 75 percent, and then forget that B runs fewer instructions. Always write every count against the same base.

37
00:08:23,496 --> 00:08:32,349
Slides 15 to 17 say which mean to use. Use the arithmetic mean for values in units of time, such as latency.

38
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:39,605
Use the harmonic mean for rates, such as throughput. A rate is inversely proportional to time.

39
00:08:40,055 --> 00:08:44,876
Use the geometric mean for ratios with no unit, such as speedup.

40
00:08:45,326 --> 00:09:10,883
Here is why the harmonic mean is correct for rates. These numbers are an example, not from the slides. Do one task at 2 tasks per second, and one at 6 tasks per second. The total time is one half plus one sixth, which is two thirds of a second, for 2 tasks. The real rate is 3 tasks per second. The harmonic mean gives 3. The arithmetic mean gives 4, which is wrong.

41
00:09:11,333 --> 00:09:20,272
Speedup is the old time divided by the new time. It is a ratio. So for an average speedup, use the geometric mean.

42
00:09:20,722 --> 00:09:38,471
Amdahl's law answers this question: if I make one part of a program faster, how much faster is the whole program? Draw the old execution time as a bar. A fraction f of the time can use the faster mode. The rest, 1 minus f, cannot.

43
00:09:38,921 --> 00:09:48,991
Make the f part s times faster. Its time becomes f divided by s. The other part does not change. In this drawing, s is 4.

44
00:09:49,441 --> 00:09:54,753
So the new time is the old time times 1 minus f, plus f over s.

45
00:09:55,203 --> 00:10:10,307
Speedup is the old time divided by the new time. So the speedup is 1 divided by 1 minus f, plus f over s. This is Amdahl's law. You must know it, and you must be able to draw this bar to rebuild it.

46
00:10:10,757 --> 00:10:19,311
Be careful. f is a fraction of the original execution time, before the change. It is not a fraction of instructions.

47
00:10:19,761 --> 00:10:30,321
Slide 19 gives four cases. With f equal to 95 percent and s equal to 1.1, the speedup is 1.094.

48
00:10:30,771 --> 00:10:37,875
With f equal to 5 percent and s equal to 10, the speedup is only 1.047.

49
00:10:38,325 --> 00:10:49,419
Even if s is infinite, with f equal to 5 percent, the speedup is only 1.052. That is 1 divided by point 9 5.

50
00:10:49,869 --> 00:10:58,637
With f equal to 95 percent and s infinite, the speedup is 20. That is 1 divided by point 0 5.

51
00:10:59,087 --> 00:11:20,804
So the speedup can never be more than 1 divided by 1 minus f. For example, if you remove half of the time completely, the speedup is only 2. The plot shows this. Each curve rises with s, but it never passes its dashed limit. For f equal to point 5, the limit is 2. For point 9 5, it is 20.

52
00:11:21,254 --> 00:11:34,054
The take away from the slides. Make the common case fast. The part that you do not improve sets the limit. Build a balanced system, and design for average performance, not peak performance.

53
00:11:34,504 --> 00:11:49,181
Now a real midterm question. Fall 2019, question 1 part A, 4 marks. Fifty percent of a program is accelerated by a factor of 4. What is the speedup? Pause and try it.

54
00:11:49,631 --> 00:11:52,383
f is point 5, and s is 4.

55
00:11:52,833 --> 00:12:01,217
The speedup is 1 divided by point 5 plus point 5 over 4. That is 1 divided by point 6 2 5.

56
00:12:01,667 --> 00:12:03,609
The speedup is 1.6.

57
00:12:04,059 --> 00:12:09,499
Part C of the same question asks: why do micro-architects use Amdahl's law?

58
00:12:09,949 --> 00:12:27,058
A good answer: Amdahl's law shows the total gain of a change before you build it. The gain is limited by the fraction of time that the faster mode is used. So architects improve the common case first, and do not spend effort on rare cases.

59
00:12:27,508 --> 00:12:53,236
This midterm question adds a slowdown. It is from the Fall 2022 paper, question 1. In part A, multiplications take 40 percent of the original time, and they become 5 times faster. In part B, the same change makes data cache accesses 3 times slower. Cache accesses take 30 percent of the original time. Pause and try both parts.

60
00:12:53,686 --> 00:13:04,523
Part A is basic Amdahl. 1 divided by point 6 plus point 4 over 5. That is 1 divided by point 6 8, which is 1.47.

61
00:13:04,973 --> 00:13:23,149
For part B, use the general form. The new time, as a fraction of the old time, is the unchanged part plus each changed part divided by its speedup. A slowdown of 3 is a speedup of one third. So that part takes 3 times its old time.

62
00:13:23,599 --> 00:13:36,037
Draw the bars. The other work is point 3 and does not change. Multiplication goes from point 4 to point 0 8. Cache accesses go from point 3 to point 9.

63
00:13:36,487 --> 00:13:54,727
The new time is point 3 plus point 0 8 plus point 9, which is 1.28. The speedup is 1 divided by 1.28, which is point 7 8. This is less than 1. So the program is slower, and the optimization is not useful.

64
00:13:55,177 --> 00:14:12,969
Assignment 1, question 1 uses Amdahl's law two times. Application A uses 80 percent of the time, and application B uses 20 percent. 40 percent of A and 99 percent of B can be made 2 times faster. Pause and try it.

65
00:14:13,419 --> 00:14:21,483
Part a. First, find the speedup of A alone. f is point 4 and s is 2. The speedup of A is 1.25.

66
00:14:21,933 --> 00:14:40,685
Then use this result as s for the system. For the system, f is point 8 and s is 1.25. The system speedup is 1.19. The bars show it. Inside app A, only the blue part becomes 2 times shorter. App B does not change.

67
00:14:41,135 --> 00:14:58,137
Part b. The speedup of B alone is 1 divided by point 0 1 plus point 9 9 over 2, which is 1.98. For the system, f is point 2 and s is 1.98. The system speedup is only 1.11.

68
00:14:58,587 --> 00:15:11,707
Part c. B is almost fully optimized, but optimizing A gives the larger gain. The reason is that A has the larger fraction of time. Choose the change with the largest f.

69
00:15:12,157 --> 00:15:18,088
The method: find the speedup of the inner part first. Then use it as s at the outer level.

70
00:15:18,538 --> 00:15:35,285
Assignment 1, question 2 is a trap. The enhanced mode is 10 times faster. It is used 50 percent of the time, but this percentage is of the new execution time, not the old one. Find f, and find the speedup. Pause and try it.

71
00:15:35,735 --> 00:15:44,929
Use a simple unit. Let the new time be 1. Half of it, point 5, is in enhanced mode. The other point 5 is not enhanced.

72
00:15:45,379 --> 00:15:57,625
Now undo the speedup. Before the change, the enhanced part took point 5 times 10, which is 5. The other part stays point 5. So the old time is 5.5.

73
00:15:58,075 --> 00:16:09,083
f is the fraction of the old time that was converted. That is 5 divided by 5.5, which is 10 over 11, or 90.9 percent.

74
00:16:09,533 --> 00:16:25,469
The speedup is the old time divided by the new time. That is 5.5 divided by 1, which is 5.5. Amdahl's law with f equal to 10 over 11 and s equal to 10 gives the same result.

75
00:16:25,919 --> 00:16:37,332
Rule: when the percentage is given on the new time, set the new time to 1. Multiply the fast part by s to get the old time. Then use the definitions.

76
00:16:37,782 --> 00:16:46,870
Here is the recap. One. Execution time is I C times CPI times the cycle time. Compare all three terms.

77
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:55,875
Two. The CPI is a weighted average over instruction types. To test a change, change one row and recalculate.

78
00:16:56,325 --> 00:17:02,085
Three. If the instruction count also changes, write every count against one base.

79
00:17:02,535 --> 00:17:07,868
Four. n times faster means the slow time divided by the fast time is n.

80
00:17:08,318 --> 00:17:21,630
Five. Amdahl's law. Speedup is 1 divided by 1 minus f, plus f over s. f is a fraction of the original time. The limit is 1 divided by 1 minus f.

81
00:17:22,080 --> 00:17:29,995
Six. With several parts or a slowdown, add the unchanged part and each changed part divided by its speedup.

82
00:17:30,445 --> 00:17:36,589
Seven. If the percentage is given on the new time, set the new time to 1 and work backwards.

83
00:17:37,039 --> 00:17:47,172
Eight. Use the arithmetic mean for times, the harmonic mean for rates, and the geometric mean for ratios. That is the end of video 2.
